A||||29||Adrift||Is this normal? (strange PIC  Inside of rear wheelwell area)||||4||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-20-2001||06:28 PM||||I took off my rear wheels for the first time today and this is the condition I found the plunger-like thing by my suspension (What is it called) in!!! How much would it cost to get something like this replaced, and how would I go about doing it?<BR>Thanks, Ari<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1669.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||California Kid||12-20-2001||06:37 PM||||That's your outer Constant Velocity (CV) joint, it's supposed to be packed with grease, but your seal boot is diffently terminal!!!!  Better replace it before the joint fails, if it already hasn't. Visit local parts supply shop.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||12-20-2001||06:41 PM||||How much does it cost to replace? And how do you know if the joint has failed?<P>I purchased the car from someone like this - I was royally screwed - There's already hundreds of dollars of other repairs, and if this is more then $50 -well, that will be the last straw.||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||12-20-2001||07:13 PM||||If the joint isn't making any noise, it might be OK. You can buy want they call a "split boot" w/grease, and clamps for under $50 from an auto parts store. It's not the best, but will work if installed properly. The problem I see in your situation is that it appears this boot has been torn for a long time, which means that somehow you have to clean the entire joint of all old grease/contaminants, before putting the new one on. If you're not sure how to do this, you better buy a book from the same place you get the parts, too many words to do it here. You can also buy a "solid boot" if you plan on removing the half shaft for this operation.<P>To have someone do it for you, is going to cost a lot more than $50.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Adrift||12-20-2001||07:31 PM||||As you can see there are no rear brakes on the car so I can't take it out for a spin to see if the joints are OK/.||||reg||1
Z||000005||David Bartlett||12-20-2001||07:44 PM||||Adrift... Just to let you know, I've seen replacment boots at Canadian tire.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GTDude||12-20-2001||08:00 PM||||Alot of parts stores you can buy the whole axle for somewhere around $89.95 US.  From experience......that's the route I'd go.<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||dgroves||12-20-2001||09:07 PM||||Just replaced both axles in my car. Whole axle with both C/V joints installed was $59.99 at Auto Zone. $20.00 core charge so take them the old ones. Lifetime gurantee too. Bad news is that you have to remove the whole bearing carrier to install.||||reg||12
Z||000008||Blacktree||12-20-2001||10:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You can buy want they call a "split boot" w/grease...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Ditto. Just a word of advice, though. Although you may be able to find a split boot for the outer CV joint (which is what Adrift needs), Speedi-boot does NOT make a split boot that fits the inner CV joints. The auto parts store will look it up, find a (wrong) part number, and unwittingly sell you an inner boot that won't fit your car. I speak from experience.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Adrift||12-21-2001||12:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Bad news is that you have to remove the whole bearing carrier to install.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is this something that's very hard to do? Would a novice be able to acomplish this with the right tools, a lot of free time and a haynes manual?||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||12-21-2001||12:26 AM||||I haven't seen something that scary since I accidentally see old wedding pictures!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously though, I'd be surprised if you said that you didn't hear any "clicking" while turning with that car. If that CV joint is still breathing it's a miracle.<P>I wouldn't drive it unless you plan on being stranded. It's gonna be a couple hundred bucks unless you are creative in the garage, have done that repair before, and have all the right tools. <P>Take care and good luck!<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000011||voyagerspe||12-21-2001||12:39 AM||||This is not a hard fix,  I would buy the whole axle assembly try <A HREF="http://www.cvunlimited.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cvunlimited.com</A> <BR>or your local auto part store probably 79.00 dollars.  just remove the brakes etc. get a small pry bar or a big screwdriver and pry the axle from the tranny.  replace with the new one.  I just changed mine in my 88 it took 15 min . a side.  If your nervous go buy a chiltons or a haynes manual it walks you through it.||||reg||14
Z||000012||Will||12-21-2001||01:24 AM||||You don't need a <I>new</I> axle. GM axles in good condition are ridiculously reliable. The local junkyard hardly ever sells ANY. A junkyard unit with no visible traumas is almost certainly OK, and should cost about $30 (certainly less from a U-Pull-It).<BR>You'll need a 24 mm socket for the camber bolts, and a T50(?) torx to remvoe calipers, and maybe a T60(?) torx to remove the wheel bearing bolts. The other tools you need are common. Hone your removal skills at the yard where breaking the occasional incidental part is OK, and working on your car will be easier.<BR>Beretta, Corsica, Sunbird, Cimarron (?), (was there a Buick L-body?), all have compatible left axles. Just be sure to find a donor car which has the same kind of trans as yours (auto or manual).||||reg||1
Z||000013||artherd||12-21-2001||06:35 AM||||I sure as heck would NOT ride on those control arms either....<P>And the frame needs rust repairs before it will be safe.<P>Get GM axels, the parts-store brands are crap unfortunately. (get entirely new axels, don't mess with anything, not worth it.)<P>Good luck man!  YES you can do it! Haynes manual will show you everything you need, watch out when pulling the axels, drain the tranny FIRST! :P<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000014||Adrift||12-21-2001||01:23 PM||||Is it really that unsafe in its current state?<BR>Here are some other pictures:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1671.JPG"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1670.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||brharris27370||12-21-2001||04:47 PM||||Whew man that is one nast looking undercarriage.  Good luck with that beast.  From the looks of just the rust on that axle nut your going to have FUN.  Hopefully that rust on the carriage isn't as bad as it looks.  A little sand paper and some paint would make it look better and last longer.  I would definitely replace those axles.  Cheap remanufactured ones are a hell of a lot better than those.  On the brakes you might want to run the beretta brake conversion on all four corners instead of trying to restore the original brakes.  Just loose your ebrake but it does stop a lot better than stock.  But it looks like your in for a lot of work.  Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||12-21-2001||04:59 PM||||That's surface rust. No structural integrety looks compromized... YET. Bes bet is to scratch a bad spot with a hard metal object;  corner of a file, or chisel... If it's shiny underneath, then it's fine, just Ooogly. A wire brush on a drill will work wonders. scrape off the nasties, untill it's vaguely shiny. Then get some muriatric acid (sp?) and brush on. Hose it off, let it dry, and slap a nice coat of roofing tar, or under-carriage rubber coating. That SHOULD halt the rust. Careful that it's PERFECTLY dry. Let it dry for a couple of days to be sure. You don't want water trapped anywhere. Also, dissassemble as much as possible to ensure maximum coverage.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000017||FieroRacer||12-21-2001||05:14 PM||||I'd recommend if you can handle it, just getting a rebuilt axle.  I had a torn boot from the previous owner in my daytona.  When I took it out, the axle broke into three peices at each of the joints.||||reg||1
Z||000018||artherd||12-21-2001||08:14 PM||||You'll have to look in person, but it seems the front cradle is rusted THROUGH...<P>Rusting weakens parts, if there's 'silver' beneth, there can still be far to little metal leaft to hold any torque.<P>You'll need at least a NEW cradle, don't try to repair this one.<P>I'd look long and hard at those a-arms too, then visit a junkyard and get a set that aren't totally messed up.<P>Reember, everything on a production automobile is basically thin-sheet steel.<P>There's not a lot of 'extra' metal there that you can spare!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000019||Adrift||12-21-2001||08:47 PM||||Ben,<BR>GM doesn't carry Axels for the fiero anymore!||||reg||1
Z||000020||cadaver||12-22-2001||05:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B><BR>Seriously though, I'd be surprised if you said that you didn't hear any "clicking" while turning with that car. If that CV joint is still breathing it's a miracle.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Only front wheel drive cars "click" when turning with bad joints.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||yo_sweet||12-22-2001||10:07 PM||||adrift i have a parts car im willing to help you out if you would like to pull the one off mine you can have it .ill even meet you theres no strange looks from the neighbors<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
