Z||000023||FiXXXerX||12-22-2001||10:40 PM||||Adrift: I've recently purchased a Fiero for $200 bux and am having some fun finding parts and getting everything fixed up as well. First thing I had trouble with was fuel, and, well, is still what I got trouble with. But when GM stops carrying them, and if you know what your looking for.... call around to local Junkyards and see if they have any Fieros in decent condition. Then see if you can get the parts off of them or if they'll get um for you and if you can come and pick um up. Thats what I've been doing with alot of the parts, cause there is a perfectly fine Fiero in this local junkyard that the guys were DRIVING around, but, since it had a nasty engine miss they just parked it until I came along, now, I got TONS of damn near perfect parts for the lowest possible price. Try that before you go with the real expensive alternative, especially with a car that might have more problems than you can see. So, if they have halfway decent parts at your local junkyard, try getting the parts from there first, especially if your car might not be in good shape in other ares. BTW, how much did you pay for it and what year and model is it?<P>Adam<P>------------------<BR>-= If the Front wheels don't leave the ground, its still broken =-||||reg||1
Z||000024||yo_sweet||12-22-2001||10:58 PM||||yeah that will be fine , i might even be able to go and take it all off, you still need the vents?, take a look at your car "internals" joint's,in case you need something else it's here id rather give it to someone who needs it then to send it to the metal yard , i know what it's like to get ripped off by people, so if there's anything else i should have it on the parts car..<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">   <P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html</A> <BR>yo_sweet!<p>[This message has been edited by yo_sweet (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Adrift||12-22-2001||11:09 PM||||Warning: Here comes the needed part list!<P>- trim for headlight (Black plastic that goes around the front of them)<BR>- body panel which goes around windshield<BR>- left and right power mirror<BR>- handles & locks for outside of both passenger and drivers door<BR>- Plastic and door lock switch which surroud inside door handle<BR>- ACTUAL door locks( the ones INSIDE the door, or at least the metal from the lock which goes from the lock to to the door lock switch inside and outi)<BR>-2 Headlight Sockets<BR>-Vents from the dashboard<P>I might have those wheels for you - I got some from my guy wouldn't surprise me if they have holes though (He has already cheated me every step of the way so it only makes sense he would do so with the wheels) Is there anywhere I can go to get the wheels checked?||||reg||1
Z||000026||yo_sweet||12-22-2001||11:21 PM||||it's easy to repair tires that have holes i have a whole kit and ive done it before so it's not that bad, i should have most parts your looking for , the whole dashboard is out so that wil maket hings easyer , the locks are gone but the rest of the setup is there, like i said whatever is there you need you can take all the wheels are off , and the two shafts are removed from the tranny. get back to me when you come back and well work something out.! <P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
Z||000027||x-thumpr-x||12-24-2001||08:00 PM||||Adift, if you need a hand when to replace your CV joint, I'll help out (been there Done That!)  I live close to the wear the 2 Danforth's meet & I have an 85GT parts car also. I first gotta figure out what I want off it first for my other 2 fiero's.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Just developed a tranny problem on my GT. <BR>SOB!||||reg||1
Z||000028||ToddAsh||12-24-2001||08:35 PM|||| I've painted frames on old Corvettes and 55-57 Chevys that looked just as bad.I use POR-15 coating.It's amazing.Brushes on like black paint and completely hardens and seals rust.Paint rightover loose rust and it hardens so tough you can't chip it off with a hammer.I sound like a salesman for it but it's really THAT good.I'm painting the suspension parts on my frontend now and they look great.By the way I agree replacing the axle is the only way to go.Very unlikely that CV joint is any good and it's not really any more expensive to replace the whole thing. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000029||x-thumpr-x||12-24-2001||09:11 PM||||true.. I had to replace one on my 6000 once. the price of a rebuild kick was $10 cheaper then the price for a bran-new CV joint.||||reg||1
